When we compile and run the above code, in Visual Studio, we get the following error:

Error   1   The type or namespace name 'PexObserve' could not be found (are you missing an assembly reference?) C:\Users\user\Desktop\Pex_Test\ArrayList\ArrayList.Test\ContractTest.cs    143 

Error   2   The type or namespace name 'PexObserve' could not be found (are you missing an assembly reference?) C:\Users\user\Desktop\Pex_Test\ArrayList\ArrayList.Test\ContractTest.cs    152 

It seems that the error occurred due to some missing assembly reference. I'm unable to locate where the problem lies. Any ideas on how I can rectify this issue?

A: It looks you're trying to use Microsoft's Pex tool (Visual Studio Plugin), which may not be available in your Visual Studio. You cannot use Pex right away within an open Visual Studio, it should be installed as an stand alone tool separately. 

To fix this problem:
1. Download and Install Microsoft.Pex from the following link: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=24099
2. Restart Visual Studio.

After the installation and the restart, Pex should be available in the Test menu. You would need to install the Visual Studio 2017 version for Pex to work correctly.

Also, note that, in order to use Pex, your unit tests project should have Microsoft.Pex.Framework installed through NuGet and the project should be of type Class Library (.NET Framework) not Class Library (.NET Standard / .NET Core).

Another issue that might exist in your case is the reference to the PexObserve class. Are you sure that PexObserve is the real name of the class you're referring to as it's not a standard class in .NET? If this class is intended to be created by you, it should be in your project.

Last, please ensure that you have the following packages installed through NuGet


*

*Microsoft.Pex

*Microsoft.Pex.Framework 

*Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Plugin

*Microsoft.Pex.VisualStudio

This might solve the problem. If none of these steps helps, you'll might want to post this question on Microsoft's official support website.
Refer the blog post: https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/pexteam/2016/11/03/announcing-pex-v26/ for more info on Pex.
I hope it may help you out with your problem. Let me know, if you need help with any other issues.
P.S: Ensure to check your project references and NuGet packages thoroughly as well. There could be an issue with assembly references or NuGet packages.
Also, ensure the right version of Visual Studio has been used to create and modify your tests – the Pex tool is a standalone tool and not bundled with Visual Studio. Make sure Visual Studio 2017 is installed and configured.
If the problem persists, it might be beneficial to review the documentation or consult the official Microsoft Support forums for further assistance. Specifically for Pex, please see: https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/blob/master/Documentation/index.md
Again, I hope this info along with the previous response helps. If the question is still unresolved after these steps, it may be best to consult dedicated forums or support sites for more personalized assistance. 
Let me know if you have any further questions or another scenario you need help on. Do let us know the specific issue you're facing and it would be better to help you out.




