Sorry, this post is quite long already.  The contract test looks at operations on the list such as add, remove, insert, contains, indexOf, lastIndexOf, set etc. I'm trying to assert the constraints of each operation.  For example, when insert operation is performed on an empty list, the returned index should be always 0.  Similarly some constraints to be applied in different scenarios


A: Contracts help in testing to ensure that different parts of the program all agree on what the output should be given a certain input.
Here, you are testing the ArrayList class methods and trying to assert some contract for each method such as add, remove, insert, set etc.
To do this, firstly, you need to have a clear understanding of how the methods are suppose to work. For example, in add operation, the ArrayList class adds the value to the end of the list and always return the new count of items in the list.
The key part of the contract testing is the preconditions and postconditions, inputs and outputs and the relationship between different calls to the method.
Now, coming to Nunit Assert method, you can use:
Assert.AreEqual / Assert.AreNotEqual : To test if two objects are equal.
Assert.IsTrue / Assert.IsFalse : To test if the condition is true or false.
For your case, you can use Assert.AreEqual / Assert.AreNotEqual to test the count of the list after or before operations. Also use Assert.IsTrue / Assert.IsFalse for any operations which should return a boolean value.

A: Contract Testing in Unit Testing refers to a technique where a unit of work, such as a function or method, is expected to complete a task according to a given contract. 
It typically involves checking the functions behavior under certain circumstances. For example, a function might be designed to accept only positive integers, which it will do. However, what if the function accepts a negative integer? The behaviour of the function may become unpredictable. Unit tests would need to assess if the function behaves as expected when a negative number is inputted and how the function responds to that undesired input.
In the case of the ArrayList class, the ArrayList.Add() method contract could demonstrate this - the Add() method could be designed to only accept positive integers. Contract testing would therefore involve verifying that, given the contract of the method, the method still acts in an appropriate manner when given a negative integer.

Here is an example of a Unit Test, specifically a Contract test where the ArrayList Add method is tested for behaviors when a negative integer is input. Also note that this is an oversimplified example for the understanding purposed:
[TestMethod]
public void ArrayList_AddNegativeInteger_Test()
{
   string negInteger = "-1";
   try
   {
      ArrayList al = new ArrayList();
      al.Add(Convert.ToInt32(negInteger));
      Assert.Fail("Expected an ArgumentException to be thrown, but no exception was thrown.");
   }
   catch (ArgumentException ae)
   {
      // Verify that an ArgumentException is thrown when a negative integer is added.
   }

Above test is written as an Unit Test case and it is doing Contract Test in form for the case when a negative integer is passed in ArrayList.
In this test, an expectation is made that an `ArgumentException` would be thrown when a negative integer is passed to `Add` method of `ArrayList` class. The test will fail if no exception is thrown (which is not expected under the given contract), and pass otherwise. 

This approach is generally use for validating the behavior of the functions for different sets of valid and invalid inputs, it is also known as Behavior verification. For other operations like remove, get, set etc also similar type of contract testing needs to be done.
It’s quite a manual process and is done through the use of unit testing frameworks which help in writing unit tests and executing them. It isn’t automatic, and you have to be aware of the possible conditions your code could get into if you’re manually interacting with your software. So, Contract Testing isn’t automatic, its manual. 

Unit tests to the rescue, by allowing us to check various conditions on a method and ensuring that they meet our expected behavior. Unit testing methods are a great way to ensure your code functions under various conditions, and if something breaks, you’ll be able to catch it early on. 

In a nutshell, the contracts are what we'll be asserting with our tests. They're statements of what our--------------------            System.Diagnostics.Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret < Old_arrListCount);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ClearContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool Old_arrListIsReadOnly = arrList.IsReadOnly;
            bool Old_arrListIsFixedSize = arrList.IsFixedSize;
            bool Old_arrListIsSynchronized = arrList.IsSynchronized;
            int Old_arrListSynchronizedRoot = arrList.SyncRoot[0];

            arrList.Clear();

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_arrListIsReadOnly = arrList.IsReadOnly;
            bool New_arrListIsFixedSize = arrList.IsFixedSize;
            bool New_arrListIsSynchronized = arrList.IsSynchronized;
            int New_arrListSynchronizedRoot = arrList.SyncRoot[0];

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IsReadOnly", Old_arrListIsReadOnly);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IsReadOnly", New_arrListIsReadOnly);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IsFixedSize", Old_arrListIsFixedSize);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IsFixedSize", New_arrListIsFixedSize);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IsSynchronized", Old_arrListIsSynchronized);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IsSynchronized", New_arrListIsSynchronized);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_SynchronizedRoot", Old_arrListSynchronizedRoot);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_SynchronizedRoot", New_arrListSynchronizedRoot);

            Assert.AreEqual(0, arrList.Count);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_CopyToContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, object[] array, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            if (array != null)
            {
                AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(array.Length - index >= arrList.Count);
            }

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            arrList.CopyTo(array, index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);

            if (array != null)
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(arrList.Count, array.Length - index);
            }

        }
    }

}

